<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashot Msaker</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 826</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Ashot IV Bagratuni · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagratuni-dynasty">Bagratuni dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ashot Msaker, the Carnivore, was a Bagratid prince who became presiding prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> in the early 9th century. He built up the family's lands, which were divided among his sons; one son, Bagrat, received Taron, founding its line of princes. His descendant Ashot I became king of Armenia.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ashot was the son of Smbat VII Bagratuni, presiding prince of Arab-ruled Armenia and sparapet, or supreme commander, who was killed in the rebellion against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> at the Battle of Bagrevand in 775. Ashot fled from the family's lands in eastern Armenia to relatives near the sources of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a>, further from Arab power and closer to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. Silver mines there let him buy part of Arsharunik from the heirs of the Kamsarakan family, which seems to have died out after 785, and he built a new lordship around the fortress of Bagaran in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayrarat">Ayrarat</a>.</p><p>His nickname Msaker, the meat eater, is usually explained, after Nikoghayos Adonts, by his refusal to keep the fast of Lent; other readings take it as a figure for his ferocity in war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The death or exile of many princely families after Bagrevand left room for Arab emirates and for the Artsruni of Vaspurakan, but Ashot restored the Bagratids through diplomacy and marriage. In about 806 Caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> named him presiding prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, an office empty since his father's death, as a counterweight to the Artsruni and a focus of loyalty away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. In the turmoil after Harun's death in 809 he twice defeated the Muslim Jahhafids, gaining Taron, Arsharunik with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirak">Shirak</a>, Ashotz and eastern Tayk.</p><p>When Jahhaf and his son Abd al-Malik seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dvin">Dvin</a> in 813, Ashot destroyed an army of 5,000 sent against him, killing 3,000, while his brother Shapuh raided around Dvin; Abd al-Malik was then killed by the local people. Ashot was left the largest landholder among the nakharar, and he married a daughter to the Artsruni prince of Vaspurakan. According to the historian Hovhannes Draskhanakerttsi, he also refused an offer from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> to accept Chalcedonian doctrine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ashot died in 826. Joseph Laurent wrote that the proscribed and dispossessed fugitive of Bagrevand died the most powerful and most popular prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. His lands were divided between his sons: Bagrat II received Taron and Sasun and later the title of prince of princes, and Smbat VIII became sparapet with the lands around Bagaran and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a>. Smbat's son Ashot I was recognised as king of Armenia in 885.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ashot Msaker” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early 9th century the Bagratid Bagrat, son of <b>Ashot Msaker</b>, secured it as his share of the family lands, and in 826 his branch became its hereditary princes under the Abbasid Caliphate.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-taron">Principality of Taron</a></li></ul></section>
